C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not collect what churches say about the prophets and sacrifice and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes the prophets themselves teach about the worship of Israel, it gives the evidence from their own sentences, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ words, so that a reader who disagrees knows exactly where."),
("p", "The question is narrow, and the prophets raise it. Samuel asks it of a king: “Hath the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the LORD?” (1 Samuel 15:22). Isaiah has the LORD say of a crowded temple, “To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices unto me?” (Isaiah 1:11). Amos has him say, “I hate, I despise your feast days” (Amos 5:21). Hosea has him say, “For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice” (Hosea 6:6). Many readers have taken these sentences to mean that the LORD, through his prophets, set aside the sacrifices he had commanded. Others have taken them to mean that he cared only for the heart and nothing for the altar. The volume asks what each prophet condemns, in whose worship, and what he leaves standing."),
("p", "The position is this. The prophets condemn the worshipers and the way they worship, and they never condemn the worship the LORD commanded. In every indictment the offerings are named as Israel’s own, brought to the LORD’s house at the LORD’s appointment. The charge falls on what the worshiper does outside the house and on what he thinks the offering buys. The sentence is judgment on the house and the people, and not abolition of the order. The remedy the prophets name is turning back, and the offerings reappear in their own hope of a purified people. The sentences that seem to say otherwise, “mercy, and not sacrifice” and “I spake not unto your fathers … concerning burnt offerings,” are statements of rank. They say what the LORD asked first and what he asks above all."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passage, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where modern writers are named, they are named for a position the author has read in a free text, and where an objection is stated, it is stated as the objector would state it and then answered from the verses."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. The Kingdom temple of Ezekiel 40–48 and the sacrifices written there are read in Typology Commentary Volume 1; the priesthood of Aaron and Melchizedek in Typology Commentary Volume 3; the scope of the phrase the law or the prophets in Study 62 and the Law’s jurisdiction in Study 59; the Sabbath of Isaiah 58:13 and 66:23 in Study 78; Malachi 3:8–12 on giving in Study 47; and Stephen’s use of Amos 5:25–27 in Acts Commentary Volume 1. This volume cites them and does not repeat them. It reads Hebrews 10:5–10 inside Israel’s line, on the letter’s own audience markers and the covenant it quotes, and builds no doctrine for the Body of Christ from the prophets’ charges. The charges were spoken to Israel about Israel’s worship, and the volume keeps them there."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "The prophets condemn the worshipers and the way they worship, never the worship the LORD commanded. Where a passage names no sentence of abolition, the volume says so and builds none."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "The offerings the prophets condemn are named as the LORD’s own appointment, brought by Israel to the LORD’s house; the charge is never that the LORD did not command them.", "Chapters 1, 2 and 3"),
    ("2", "The charge falls on the worshiper’s conduct and on what he thinks the offering buys; the indictment is of the people who bring and the way they bring.", "Chapters 2, 3 and 5"),
    ("3", "“Mercy, and not sacrifice” and “I spake not … concerning burnt offerings” are statements of rank; the sentence is judgment on the house and the people, not abolition, and the remedy is to turn back.", "Chapters 4, 5 and 6"),
    ("4", "The offerings reappear in the prophets’ own hope; Hebrews 10 gives the same verdict from the other side, that the Law’s sacrifices never took sins away, inside Israel’s line.", "Chapters 7 and 8"),
  ], [0.12, 0.62, 0.26]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any prophetic condemnation of worship and ask four questions of it in order: whose offering is it, what does the LORD say against it, what does he do about it, and what does he leave standing? If a prophet anywhere says the order itself is wrong, or that the LORD never commanded it, the thesis fails. If each indictment instead names the offering as Israel’s own, charges the worshiper, and sentences the house and people while holding the way back open, the thesis stands, and the reader will have seen why the prophets can say hard things about sacrifice and yet speak of the offerings of a purified people."),
]
